<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ashura">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashura is the tenth day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>, the first month of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>. For Shia Muslims it is the day of mourning for the martyrdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Battle of Karbala</a> in 680, marked by processions, sermons and passion plays. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims observe it as a day of voluntary fasting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashura.jpg" alt="Ashura" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Undetermined. Original uploader was Alborzagros · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%86%D9%82%D8%A7%D8%B4%D9%8A_%D8%B8%D9%87%D8%B1_%D8%B9%D8%A7%D8%B4%D9%88%D8%B1%D8%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
